<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Weather Report – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Weather Report</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Will the Weather Change Tomorrow?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Interpret simple charts and graphs related to texts. reading_2: Write responses to questions about informational texts. phonemic_awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by segmenting multisyllabic words into syllables.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can interpret data about weather patterns in charts (e.g., 'More people like sunny days.'). Can answer questions about weather-related texts (e.g., 'What's the weather like tomorrow?' 'It will rain tomorrow.'). Can segment multisyllabic words into syllables, demonstrating knowledge of phonemic awareness (e.g., 'thermostat' into 'ther-mo-stat').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks, "What's the weather like today?" The teacher shows flashcards of various weather conditions (sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy, cold, warm) and asks students to name them. Students then say what the weather is like outside right now, using previously learned vocabulary. The teacher introduces the idea of 'weather prediction' for tomorrow.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simple weather chart for *today* with icons and temperature. The teacher points to icons and says, "Today it is sunny. The temperature is 25 degrees Celsius. It is warm." Then, the teacher points to the "forecast" section for *tomorrow* and says, "Tomorrow, it *will be* rainy. The temperature *will be* 20 degrees. It *will be* cold." The teacher emphasizes "will be" and intonation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this weather for today or tomorrow?" (Pointing to forecast section). Expected answer: "Tomorrow."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Will it be hot or cold tomorrow?" (Referring to the modeled prediction). Expected answer: "Cold."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with a short, simple weather report for two different cities (e.g., City A and City B) for 'tomorrow.' The report includes a brief text and a corresponding visual chart with weather icons (sun, cloud, rain, wind), temperature in Celsius, and time periods (morning, afternoon, evening). The text will integrate future simple tense, weather vocabulary (forecast, report, cloud, rain, wind, sun, temperature, sky, clear, dry, wet, warm, cold), and prepositions of place (in the north, over the city).<br>Example chart entry: "City A - Tomorrow Morning: ☀️ 28°C - Sunny and clear sky. Afternoon: ☁️ 25°C - Cloudy. Evening: 🌧️ 20°C - Rain will appear."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will interpret simple weather information from a written text and associated charts/graphs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher asks students to read the text and chart silently. Then, the teacher asks: "What is this report about?" "What cities are mentioned?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read 3-4 simple True/False statements about the weather report, marking their answers. E.g., "City A will be rainy in the morning. (True/False)" "City B's temperature will decrease in the evening. (True/False)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match weather icons from the chart to their corresponding weather words (e.g., matching a cloud icon to 'cloudy,' a rain icon to 'rainy').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice interpreting weather data in texts and charts, using future simple tense for predictions, weather vocabulary (e.g., increase, decrease, expect, sunshine, breeze), prepositions of place (on, at, in), and segmenting multisyllabic words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill):</b> The teacher provides sentences with gaps based on the weather report from Stage 2. Students complete the sentences using words from a word bank (e.g., "Tomorrow, the ____ in City A will be cloudy." "The rain will be ____ the lake." "The temperature will ____ to 20°C."). Focus on grammar (future simple, prepositions) and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Completion from Chart):</b> Students receive a new, incomplete weather chart (e.g., missing conditions or temperatures for certain times/cities). They use clues from a short descriptive text to complete the chart. E.g., "The forecast for Monday afternoon says it will be windy and cool." Students then draw a wind icon and write a suitable temperature like '20°C' in the chart for Monday afternoon. This reinforces chart interpretation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness (Segmenting Multisyllabic Words):</b> The teacher writes multisyllabic weather-related words on the board (e.g., 'temperature', 'afternoon', 'prediction', 'thermostat'). The teacher models segmenting "thermostat" into "ther-mo-stat" by clapping or tapping for each syllable. Students then work in pairs to segment the other words, saying them aloud. The teacher circulates, listens, and provides feedback.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will work individually or in pairs to read a new, slightly more complex weather report and chart for a different region or time period (e.g., "Weather in the Mountains This Weekend"). The report includes 3-4 days with various weather conditions, temperatures, and perhaps brief notes about activities. They will then write short answers to 4-5 comprehension questions based on the text and chart.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes a new weather report and chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the report and analyze the chart independently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides a set of questions, such as: "What will the weather be like on Saturday morning?" "What is the highest temperature expected on Sunday?" "Where will the storm appear?" "You should bring an umbrella on which day?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students write their answers using complete sentences and target grammar (future simple, prepositions of place) and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students can optionally peer-check their answers for accuracy and clarity before submission.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in reading and interpreting informational texts, collaborative interaction (if working in pairs), and communicative task completion through accurate written responses.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during pair work and review of student written responses from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written answers to the comprehension questions about the new weather report and chart. The teacher also monitors students' ability to segment multisyllabic words during Stage 3's activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of answers to reading comprehension questions (interpreting the chart and text).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (weather nouns, verbs, adjectives, prepositions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Future Simple tense in written responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempt to segment multisyllabic words correctly (observed during activity).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of written responses.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned how to read about the weather by ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new weather word I can use is ______."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading the weather report was easiest for me?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the weather report was most challenging, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., visual charts, guided questions, phonemic awareness activity).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with chart interpretation or writing responses? (e.g., more examples, simpler texts, sentence starters).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the students able to correctly identify the future simple tense in the text?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build upon the understanding of weather reports and predictions from this reading lesson. Students will use the same vocabulary and grammar to *talk* about the weather and forecast, practicing asking and answering questions about future weather conditions based on similar charts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>